{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Remember what HaShem your Elohim did to Miriam</b>. If you come to be warned not to be afflicted with tzara'at, you must not speak lashon ha-ra (evil speech) — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 24:9). In my opinion, this is an actual positive commandment, like <b>Remember the Sabbath day to sanctify it</b> (Exodus 20:8), <b>Remember this day in which you came out of Egypt</b> (Exodus 13:3), <b>Remember what Amalek did to you</b> (Deuteronomy 25:17) — all of them are commandments; if so, this too is like them, and it is a warning against speaking lashon ha-ra. He commands by way of a positive commandment that we remember the great punishment that HaShem inflicted upon the righteous prophetess, who spoke only concerning her brother, repaying the kindness with which she loved him as her own soul, and she did not speak to his face so as to shame him, nor in public, but only between herself and her holy brother in private — and none of her good deeds availed her. So too you: if you sit with your brother and speak, if you cast aspersion on the son of your mother's womb, you will not escape. And this is the language of Sifra (Bechukotai, parasha 1:2–3): When Scripture says, <b>And you shall not do all these commandments</b> (Leviticus 26:14) — behold, what is written in the Torah is stated. If so, why is it said, <b>And if you do not hearken to Me</b>? — to be diligent in Torah. And so it says: <b>Remember the Sabbath day to sanctify it</b> — one might think in your heart; when it says <b>Observe</b>, behold, guarding of the heart is stated. What then do I fulfill by <b>Remember</b>? — that you repeat it with your mouth. And so it says: <b>Remember, do not forget</b>, etc. And so it says: <b>Remember what HaShem your Elohim did to Miriam</b> — one might think in your heart; when it says <b>Take heed concerning the plague of tzara'at, to be very careful and to do</b> (Deuteronomy 24:8), behold, guarding of the heart is stated. What then do I fulfill by <b>Remember</b>? — that you repeat it with your mouth. And so it says: <b>Remember what Amalek did to you</b> — one might think in your heart; when it says <b>Do not forget</b>, behold, forgetting of the heart is stated. What then do I fulfill by <b>Remember</b>? — that you repeat it with your mouth. And its explanation according to them is: <b>Take heed concerning the plague of tzara'at</b> — from the plague of tzara'at, <b>to be very careful</b> that it not come upon you, <b>and to do</b> according to all that the priests instruct you, and <b>Remember what HaShem your Elohim did to Miriam</b> — that you keep mentioning this with your mouth always. And from here it appears that our Sages treat it as a commandment, not merely a lesson and advice to escape from plagues. And how is it possible that lashon ha-ra, which is weighed against bloodshed, should not have in the Torah a complete negative prohibition or a negative commandment derived from a positive one? Rather, in this verse there is a great warning within it, to refrain from it — whether openly or in secret, whether one intends to harm and disgrace or whether one does not intend to harm at all. And this is a commandment among the 613 commandments, and its omission is a matter for the author of the Halakhot Gedolot and all those who count the commandments after him."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:55:35.283467287Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}